WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.860
A GoPro, carried through a shopping mall.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.582
No LiDAR, no calibration target.

00:00:04.961 --> 00:00:07.874
The map on the right is built from its video and its

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.331
own IMU, as the camera walks.

00:00:11.457 --> 00:00:12.075
I'm Baseline.

00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:15.781
Accord makes dense metric maps from one camera and an IMU, and

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:18.252
every metre in them comes from one place.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.862
A visual-inertial filter knows how far the camera moved, in metres, but

00:00:23.862 --> 00:00:26.173
its map is a few hundred points.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:29.799
A depth model gives every pixel a depth, but even when it

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:33.005
is told the poses, its scale is off by a few percent.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.754
The filter tracks every frame on the CPU, and the map never

00:00:37.754 --> 00:00:38.805
writes back to it.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.116
Every half second a frame becomes a keyframe, and five of

00:00:42.116 --> 00:00:43.175
them make a window.

00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:47.608
The depth model predicts the window's depth, conditioned on the filter's poses.

00:00:48.008 --> 00:00:52.604
Keypoints triangulated under those same poses pull the window onto the filter's scale.

00:00:53.004 --> 00:00:56.639
Each window becomes a submap in a graph that loop closures correct.

00:00:58.242 --> 00:01:00.029
Six images draw the same wall.

00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:03.604
After the window fit, each window's copy sits at its own depth.

00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:06.528
The anchor gives every window the filter's scale.

00:01:06.528 --> 00:01:09.972
A few percent per image is left, and at three metres

00:01:09.972 --> 00:01:11.224
that is several centimetres.

00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:15.821
So images from different windows that see one wall are compared, both ways.

00:01:15.821 --> 00:01:19.356
One correction per image, and the copies fall onto one line.

00:01:19.776 --> 00:01:22.544
What no step can see is the filter's own scale error.

00:01:22.544 --> 00:01:24.054
The whole map moves with it.

00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.271
Here is that on a real floor plan.

00:01:28.271 --> 00:01:30.976
Before the correction, one wall is smeared into layers.

00:01:31.356 --> 00:01:35.094
After it, the layers agree, and the map's median distance to the

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:38.833
laser scan drops from two point four to one point three centimetres.

00:01:40.325 --> 00:01:43.314
On a drone in a Vicon room, the map builds around the

00:01:43.314 --> 00:01:44.809
flight while the filter keeps tracking.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.040
Scored with each map placed by its own trajectory, Accord has the

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.695
best mean map on all four scanned datasets, and the runner-up changes every time.

00:01:56.258 --> 00:02:00.112
I built the release on an RTX 3090 and ran two rooms

00:02:00.112 --> 00:02:01.718
it was never tuned on.

00:02:01.718 --> 00:02:05.251
The corrected maps landed a few hundredths from the paper's, at

00:02:05.251 --> 00:02:06.535
seventeen frames a second.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.584
The scale is the filter's.

00:02:09.584 --> 00:02:13.027
In one of my rooms the filter ran one point three percent

00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:15.035
long, and so did the corrected map.

00:02:15.455 --> 00:02:19.006
Depth past twenty metres is dropped, so the far side of a

00:02:19.006 --> 00:02:20.189
big courtyard stays empty.

00:02:20.609 --> 00:02:25.031
And on uncalibrated phones every pipeline scores far lower, Accord included.

00:02:26.308 --> 00:02:27.844
The metres come from the filter.

00:02:27.844 --> 00:02:30.659
The map's job is to make every image agree on them.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.933
Anchor to the filter, make the images agree, and the scale stays put.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.171
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:41.171 --> 00:02:41.791
I'm Baseline.

00:02:41.791 --> 00:02:42.101
Bye!
